SLASH'EM's code for this (missmu()) counts through the pieces of
armour (and magical protection) in a set order, adding the AC
contribution from each slot successively until it reaches the
threshold by which you were missed; however, gloves and boots only
have a 10% chance of being counted in for this purpose, and helmets
20%. If it doesn't reach the threshold, you get a non-specific "The foo
misses." result. It's somewhat arguable how representative this is :-)

> Isn't there reason to think that this system is in use 'inside' vanilla 
> Nethack too? Or are armor pieces that are affected by mêlée 
> rust/disenchantment/etc attacks just chosen randomly from all worn in 
> the outermost layer? Couldn't find information on this in the 
> spoilers...

Effectively, yes they are (in that relative AC contribution isn't
taken into account), but it's not strictly a straight random
choice. 

For rust (and corrosion and decay), hurtarmor() keeps picking a
straight random choice (ignoring whether there's anything actually
there) from helmet, outermost-body-armour, shield, gloves, and boots
until it _either_ successfully rusts something _or_ gets a body armour
result, rustprone or not.

For disenchantment, some_armor() starts with the earliest present in
the list of outermost-body-armour, helmet, gloves, boots, and shield,
then moves down the rest of the list, with a 1/4 chance of changing
its mind to each slot that's filled in succession. Once it reaches the
end of the list, whatever it's come up with is the piece of armour
picked to affect. This is the same system that's used for scrolls of
enchant armour, scrolls and monster-cast spells of destroy armour,
passive acid attacks, and acid blasts.

